## DigitalOcean Signals points reviewers to specific agent conversation steps

Source: DigitalOcean — https://www.digitalocean.com/blog/digitalocean-signals-observability-ai-agents
Published: 2026-10-28
Channel: news

DigitalOcean Signals examines production agent conversations to help teams decide which sessions deserve review. Its October 28 article describes a public preview for Managed Agents and Inference Engine. Instead of asking another language model to grade every conversation, Signals detects behavioral patterns such as repeated tool attempts, user corrections and external API errors. Each signal includes a run, step, confidence score and snippet.

For illustration, an assistant might finish its run successfully after repeatedly retrying a failing command. A completion metric would miss that experience; a retry signal gives a reviewer a specific step to inspect. DigitalOcean describes this as first-pass triage, and a flag does not establish that an answer was wrong. Sessions can also contain useful positive examples.

Analysis is off by default and applies to conversations beginning after consent. Reports update after inactivity and closure; dashboard metrics can lag by up to an hour. Selected sessions can become evaluation datasets and simulation scenarios. Exports redact personal information in free text, but the article says scenario creation uses the non-redacted version before redacting displayed content. Teams handling sensitive conversations should examine that distinction before enabling the preview.

Why it matters: For teams running DigitalOcean agents: evaluate signal usefulness on a small consented workload, and review export and scenario privacy behavior before expanding access.

