## LangChain tests whether stable evidence paths make agent answers repeatable

Source: LangChain — https://www.langchain.com/blog/building-agent-memory-openwiki-graphs
Published: 2026-10-28
Channel: news

OpenWiki compiles source documents into a linked wiki that agents can reuse as memory. LangChain’s October 28 experiment asks whether that memory also makes answers easier to reproduce and audit. It tests a synthetic insurance library containing 111 documents and a controlled ledger of 972 facts, with 80 lookup questions and 50 questions requiring connections across documents. Each question runs three times.

The important distinction is between correct answers and consistent evidence. The study scores accuracy against the fact ledger, while citation consistency measures overlap between cited sources across repeated runs. A model could repeatedly cite the same wrong passage, so consistency alone is not correctness. For illustration, an insurance assistant answering a coverage question may need both an original policy and a later amendment; checking only its final answer can hide a changing evidence path.

LangChain adds a graph linking wiki claims to specific supporting paragraphs and related documents. It reports higher accuracy and citation consistency for connected questions, while simple lookups remain as accurate and faster without the wiki. Reported answer costs exclude compilation and updates. This is a publisher-run synthetic experiment, not independent proof of production gains.

Why it matters: For retrieval evaluation owners: measure evidence stability alongside answer accuracy, and include memory maintenance costs when testing whether a compiled wiki is worthwhile.

